course: dictionary
lesson: 11440
title: A formal word: adduce
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Adduce is a formal word, often found in law, essays and old books. It means to give something as a reason or proof, so that it supports what you say. «The lawyer adduced three witnesses to show the shop was shut that night.» Witnesses are people who saw something, so adduced must mean brought forward as proof.
explain: Look at the words after adduce. They often name the thing being given: evidence, reasons, examples or facts. «She adduced clear examples to show her idea worked.» The examples are what she brought forward, so adduce is about backing up a point.
explain: In everyday talk, people say give, point to, or bring up instead. Try the swap: «She gave clear examples to show her idea worked.» The sentence still makes sense, so you have understood adduce. When a word feels stiff, use a plainer word from the same sentence.
fact: Adduce means to give something as a reason or proof.
fact: Adduce is a formal word, found in law, essays, exam answers and old books.
fact: Common phrases are «adduce evidence», «adduce reasons» and «adduce examples».
fact: The other forms of the verb are adduces, adduced and adducing.
fact: Everyday synonyms include give, point to and bring up.
exercise: «The detective adduced the muddy boots as proof that Sam had been in the garden.» What does adduced mean here? | Hidden from view | Washed clean | Lost somewhere | *Gave as proof
exercise: Mia wrote, «I adduced three examples to support my point in the essay.» What did Mia do? | Forgot her point | Ate her lunch early | *Gave examples to support her point | Wrote a poem about trees
exercise: «In court, the lawyer adduced a photograph and two letters.» Which everyday words could replace adduced? | *Brought up | Lost | Burned | Hid
test: «The scientist adduced data from ten experiments to prove her idea.» What does adduced mean? | Hidden in a drawer | Forgotten about | Thrown away | *Brought forward as proof
test: «The teacher asked him to adduce ___ for his answer.» Which word fits the gap best? | *reasons | puddles | glue | sandwiches
exam: «He adduced three facts to back up his claim.» Which everyday words could replace adduced in this sentence? | Break and drop | Hide and forget | *Give and point to | Dance and sing
